+version: "1.0.0"
+description: "Secret source that keeps provider credentials in one AES-256-GCM encrypted file instead of plaintext .env: a 32-byte key file unlocks the store at startup and the Hermes secret orchestrator applies the values, so precedence and provenance behave like any other source. Ships `hermes sealed init/set/unset/list/status/rotate`; values are read from a prompt or stdin and are never printed or passed in argv. Disclosure — no network, no helper CLI, no subprocesses. Reads the configured store and key file only (default $HERMES_HOME/secrets/sealed.{store,key}, both 0600); refuses to load when a file is group/world readable if secrets.sealed.strict_permissions is on. The CLI writes those two paths (plus .bak copies on init --force and rotate); values are entered by prompt or stdin. By default sealed values overwrite .env / shell values for the same variable (secrets.sealed.override_existing). No telemetry, no self-updater, no daemon. Requires the `cryptography` package, declared as a plugin dependency."
